A||N||8||MontanaFiero||Change Wheel Sizes||||5||
Z||000000||MontanaFiero||02-07-2001||03:28 AM||||My '86 SE has no wheels but of course was shipped with the 14" aluminum rims. I am seriously considering going with GT Lace rims. If I'm not mistaken, aren't those 15" rims?<P>So am I going to have to mess with the speedometer to compensate for the different tire size?<P>If so, whose done this-- I can solder, I just need instructions.||||reg||5
Z||000001||RossT||02-07-2001||03:33 AM||||You can go to the 15 inch lace rims without any problems.  Use this link to calculate you demensions.  <A HREF="http://www.secret-secret.com/turbo/DIY/tirecalc/turbotire.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.secret-secret.com/turbo/DIY/tirecalc/turbotire.html</A> <P>several on the forum have not had to change the spedo when they went to the 15 inch from the 14 inch. You can change the tire size to keep the overal diameter the same.  Do a search under "oreif" he has many pictures of his set up.||||reg||1
Z||000002||RossT||02-07-2001||03:37 AM||||Here is one link where "ofeif" talks about tire-rim size and speedo stuff<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/004176.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/004176.html</A> <P>BTW welcome to the forum, I think you are the first from Montana!!  Ever get out this way??||||reg||1
Z||000003||MontanaFiero||02-07-2001||03:52 AM||||RossT<BR>Yeah, I went through bismark late last summer on my way to  Minneapolis. I was driving a maroon Fiero and I even got behind another Fiero in "heavy" traffic. Maybe it was you. I dunno. <P>All I know is I blew the head gasket on that trip and destroyed my windshield with all that chip sealin' that they were doing.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||02-07-2001||10:26 AM||||Follow the link RossT put up.<BR>SE's look nice with GT style wheels!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1986SE.jpg">   <P><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Will||02-07-2001||04:50 PM||||If you get the VSS from a car which came with the 15" wheels your speedo will read correctly AND you can use the right size tires.||||reg||1
Z||000006||MontanaFiero||02-07-2001||05:07 PM||||So, it's the VSS, not a capacitor or resistor change on the spedometer itself?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Oreif||02-07-2001||06:18 PM||||85 GT, 86-87 SE came with 14" wheels<BR>86 thru 88 GT and 88 Formula came with 15" wheels.<BR>The VSS Sensor is the same part# for all cars. The difference is the gear color. (number of teeth on the gear) Use the tire calculator at the above link and All you have to do is type in the stock wheel size, get the overall diameter and stay within 1% of the size. The speedometer accuracy isn't a big deal at 1% (so at 100 mph you are doing either 99 or 101) Most speedos can be off up to 2% from the factory anyhow. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Will||02-08-2001||12:12 AM||||Why the negative opinion about the VSS gear change? I can go out to my car right now and have the VSS gear in my hand in 2 minutes! Changing the gear is a non-issue. The gears have their own part numbers which can be looked up, and the gear is all that's necessary to change. I only said VSS for the sake of simplicity.<P>If you use lower profile tires to keep your speedo accurate without changing the VSS gear, you will be sacrificing gas mileage. The taller tires <I>do</I> make a difference there. You will also have a large wheel house gap which would be smaller and look much better with the taller tires (Oreif's car looks good because it has been lowered as well). You will also have more ground clearance with the taller tires, which can make a difference on rough urban roads and parking lots.<BR>Having your speedo right with stock sized tires also gives you a little more room to play with larger than stock tires, such as 225,235,or even 245/60-15's. I have (cheap) 245/60-15 tires on the back of my '87 GT. It corners well for what I spent on tires, looks good, and gets almost 30 mpg on the highway (5spd). The speedo reads ~4 mph low at 65 or so.||||reg||1
